{{also|Conjunction}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*yewg-}}
From {{der|en|fro|conjonction}}, from {{der|en|la|coniūnctiō||joining}}, from {{m|la|coniungere||to join}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kənˈd͡ʒʌŋk.ʃn̩/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-conjunction.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyphenation|en|con|junc|tion}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌŋkʃən|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{examples|sense=grammar|* ''Bread, butter '''and''' cheese.''}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|act or condition of joining or being joined}} The [[act]] of [[join]]ing, or [[condition]] of being joined.
#: {{synonyms|en|connection|union}}
#* {{RQ:Eliot Middlemarch|book=I|chapter=XVIII|passage={{...|If Mr. Bulstrode insisted, as he was apt to do, on the Lutheran doctrine of justification, as that by which a Church must stand or fall,}} Dr. Minchin in return was quite sure that man was not a mere machine or a fortuitous '''conjunction''' of atoms; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1896|title=Familiar Studies of Men and Books|author=w:Robert Louis Stevenson|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/425/425-h/425-h.htm|passage=About them all there is that sort of stiff quaint unreality, that '''conjunction''' of the grotesque, and even of a certain bourgeois snugness, with passionate contortion and horror, that is so characteristic of Gothic art.}}
# {{senseid|en|grammar}} {{lb|en|grammar}} A [[word]] used to join other words, [[phrase#English:_grammar|phrase]]s{{,}} or [[clause]]s together into sentences. {{ng|(The specific conjunction used shows how the two joined parts are related [[semantic]]ally.)}}
#: {{hypo|en|coordinating conjunction|subordinating conjunction}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1881|title=The Verbalist|author=Alfred Ayres [pseudonym; Thomas Embly Osmun]|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/22457/22457-h/22457-h.htm|passage=A comma is placed between short members of compound sentences, connected by ''and'', ''but'', ''for'', ''nor'', ''or'', ''because'', ''whereas'', ''that'' expressing purpose (so that, in order that), and other '''conjunctions'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|cooccurrence or coincidence}} {{U|cooccurrence}}; [[coincidence]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1843|title=w:A System of Logic|volume=1|author=w:John Stuart Mill|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/26495/26495-h/26495-h.html|passage={{...|But where it is seen what the phenomenon is, there is seldom any difficulty in seeing that the assertion conveyed by the proposition is,}} the coexistence of one such phenomenon with another; or the succession of one such phenomenon to another: their '''''conjunction''''', in short, so that where the one is found, we may calculate on finding both.}}
# {{senseid|en|alignment of celestial bodies, in astronomy}} {{lb|en|astronomy}} The alignment of two bodies in the [[solar system]] such that they have the same [[longitude]] when seen from Earth.
#: {{antonyms|en|opposition}}
#: {{hyponyms|en|grand conjunction|great conjunction|inferior conjunction|superior conjunction|topocentric conjunction}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|title=Starf*cker{{...|: The Catastrophic Conjunction of Venus and Mars}}|publisher=Ev Cochrane|isbn=9780977285129|page=140|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PyftdgOHwtAC&lpg=PA60&dq=%22conjunction%22%20stars&hl=&pg=PA140#v=onepage&q=%22conjunction%22&f=false|text=The spectacular '''conjunction''' of Venus and Mars gave rise to a myriad of mythical interpretations.}}
# {{senseid|en|aspect type, in astrology}} {{lb|en|astrology}} An [[aspect]] in which planets are in close proximity to one another.
# {{senseid|en|proposition type, in logic}} {{lb|en|logic}} The [[proposition]] resulting from the combination of two or more propositions using the [[∧]] (<math>\and</math>) [[operator]].
#: {{meronyms|en|conjunct|logical connective}}
#: {{cot|en|disjunction}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Eliezer Yudkowsky|title=Conjunction Fallacy|magazine=w:LessWrong|url=https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/QAK43nNCTQQycAcYe/conjunction-fallacy|date=2007-09-19|passage=However, sequence 1 ''dominates'' sequence 2, because sequence 1 is strictly included in 2.  2 is 1 preceded by a G; that is, 2 is the '''conjunction''' of an initial G with 1.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260203072222/https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/QAK43nNCTQQycAcYe/conjunction-fallacy}}
# {{senseid|en|place where multiple things meet}} A place where multiple things meet.
#: {{cot|en|junction|juncture|confluence}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=David L. Lieber; Jules Harlow|title=Etz Hayim: Torah and Commentary|page=47|text=Today there is a mountain called Ararat near the '''conjunction''' of the Turkish, Armenian, and Iranian borders.}}
# {{senseid|en|sexual intercourse, obsoletely}} {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[sexual intercourse|Sexual intercourse]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:copulation}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|volume=1|chapter=29|passage=Certaine Nations (and amongst others, the Mahometane) abhorre '''Conjunction''' with women great with childe.}}

