# {{lb|en|astronomy}} An [[arbitrary]] [[formation]] of [[star#Noun|stars]] [[perceive]]d as a [[figure#Noun|figure]] (especially one from [[mythology]]) or [[pattern#Noun|pattern]], or a [[division]] of the [[sky#Noun|sky]] [[include|including]] it, especially one [[officially]] [[recognize]]d by [[astronomer]]s; an [[asterism]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Isaiah|13|9–10|page=772|column=2|passage=Behold, the day of the {{smallcaps|Lord}} commeth, cruell both with wrath and fierce anger, to lay the land deſolate; and he ſhall deſtroy the ſinners thereof out of it. For the ſtarres of heauen, and the '''conſtellations''' thereof ſhall not giue their light: the ſunne ſhalbe darkened in his going forth, and the moone ſhall not cauſe her light to ſhine.}}
#* {{RQ:Donne Poems|poem=Epithalamion|stanza=III|page=119|passage=Up, up, faire Bride, and call, / Thy ſtarres, from out their ſeverall boxes, take / Thy Rubies, Pearles, and Diamonds forth, and make / Thy ſelfe a '''conſtellation''', of them All.|footer=A figurative use.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Marcus Manilius|translator=w:Edward Sherburne|title=The Sphere of Marcus Manilius Made an English Poem:{{nb...|With Annotations and an Astronomical Appendix.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Nathanael Brooke,{{nb...|at the sign of the Angel in Cornhil, near the Royal Exchange}}|year=1675|page=25|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KGFDAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA25|oclc=https://www.worldcat.org/title/4280892|passage=Next the cold Bears, (the Cauſe t' himſelf beſt knovvn) / Shines forth a kneeling '''Conſtellation'''. / Behind vvhoſe Back ''Arctophylax'' appears, / The ſame ''Boötes'' call'd, becauſe yoak'd Steers / He ſeeming drives; vvho through the rapid Skies / (Bearing ''Arcturus'' in his Boſome) hies.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Illustrations of the Tenth Plate. Taurus.—Orion.—Gemini.—Canis Minor.—Harpa Georgii.|title=Astronomical Recreations; or Sketches of the Relative Position and Mythological History of the Constellations|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=Anthony Finley,{{nb...|N.E. corner of Fourth and Chesnut Streets. Clark & Razer, Printers, 33 Carter’s Alley.}}|year=1824|page=73|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=oPReAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA73|oclc=950910684|passage={{smallcaps|Harpa Georgii}}, or the Harp of George, is a new '''constellation''' introduced on the maps by one of the German astronomers, in honour of the late king of England, {{w|George III}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Emerson Complete Works|volume=I|page=84|passage=Who can doubt that poetry will revive and lead in a new age, as the star in the '''constellation''' Harp, which now flames in our zenith, astronomers announce, shall one day be the pole-star for a thousand years?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Francis Reddy|title=Spitzer Finds Supernova’s Echo|journal=Astronomy|location=Waukesha, Wis.|publisher=[[w:Kalmbach Media|Kalmbach Publishing Company]]|date=9 June 2005|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230406133230/https://astronomy.com/news/2005/06/spitzer-finds-supernovas-echo|section=image caption|issn=0091-6358|oclc=909718345|passage=Located 11,000 light-years away in the northern '''constellation''' Cassiopeia, Cas A is the remnant of a once massive star that died in a violent explosion 325 years ago.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Rick Barrett|title=Dairy farmers are in crisis—and it could change Wisconsin forever|journal=w:USA Today|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230410061038/https://www.usatoday.com/in-depth/news/special-reports/dairy-crisis/2019/02/21/wisconsin-dairy-farms-failing-milk-prices-fall/2540796002/|location=McLean, Va.|publisher=[[w:Gannett|Gannett Co.]]|date=21 February 2019|issn=0734-7456|oclc=658972642|passage=There was a time when the soft glow of barn lights dotted Wisconsin’s rural landscape like stars in a '''constellation''', connecting families who labored into the night milking cows, feeding calves and finishing chores.}}
