=====Verb=====
{{wikidata lexeme|L12995}}
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To form (a resulting mixture) by combining different elements, ingredients, or parts; to [[mingle]] with something else.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:mix}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''compound''' a medicine}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|IV|v|passage=Only '''compound''' me with forgotten dust.}}
#* {{RQ:Spectator|V|411|21 June 1712|passage=We have the power of altering{{...}} and '''compounding''' those images{{...}} into all the varieties of picture.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Ivanhoe|passage=incapacitating him from successfully '''compounding''' a tale of this sort}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|author=Harry E. Wedeck|title=Dictionary of Aphrodisiacs|publisher=The Citadel Press|location=New York|page=62|passage=In eighteenth century France, many aphrodisiac dishes and pastries were '''compounded''' with chocolate.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|legal}} To [[settle]] by agreeing on less than the claim, or on different terms than those stipulated.
#: {{syn|en|settle}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''compound''' a debt}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[settle]] amicably; to adjust by agreement.
#: {{syn|en|compromise}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|II|i|passage=I pray, my lords, let me '''compound''' this strife.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1777|author=w:Richard Brinsley Sheridan|title=The School for Scandal|section=II.iii
|passage=No! no—if Charles has done nothing false or mean, I shall '''compound''' for his extravagance}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come to terms of agreement; to settle by a compromise.
#: {{syn|en|agree|Thesaurus:agree}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''compound''' with someone / for something}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Measure|IV|ii|passage=Here's a fellow will help you to-morrow; {{...}} '''compound''' with him by the year.}}
#* {{RQ:Clarendon History|passage=They were at last glad to '''compound''' for his bare commitment to the Tower.}}
#* {{RQ:Carew Survey of Cornwall|passage=[Cornwall] '''compounded''' to furnish ten oxen after Michaelmas for thirty pounds.}}
#* {{RQ:Butler Hudibras|year=1905|passage='''Compound''' for sins they are inclined to / By damning those they have no mind to.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[compose]]; to [[constitute]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:compose}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|IV|ii|passage=his pomp and all what state '''compounds'''}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|finance}} To increase in value with [[interest]], where the interest is earned on both the [[principal]] sum and prior earned interest.
# {{lb|en|transitive|see usage notes}} To [[worsen]] a situation.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:make matters worse}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020-04-12|author=Simon Tisdall|title=US's global reputation hits rock-bottom over Trump's coronavirus response|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/apr/12/us-global-reputation-rock-bottom-donald-trump-coronavirus|journal=w:The Guardian|passage={{...|Europeans were already outraged by Trump’s reported efforts to acquire monopoly rights to a coronavirus vaccine under development in Germany.}} This latest example of nationalistic self-interest '''compounded''' anger across the EU over Trump’s travel ban, imposed last month without consultation or scientific justification.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013070453/https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/apr/12/us-global-reputation-rock-bottom-donald-trump-coronavirus|archivedate=13 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|horse racing|intransitive}} Of a horse: to fail to maintain speed.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1855|title=The Sporting Review|volume=34|page=240
|passage=At the hill, the Warrior must have been at least ten lengths in front of Wild Dayrell; but he '''compounded''' about 200 yards on the T. Y. C. side of the Red House.}}

======Usage notes======
The usage in sense 7 above, “to worsen a situation” is widespread but not wholly accepted.  The original meaning of the word (see senses 2–4) implies resolution of a problem, not worsening.  It has been suggested (Fraser 1973) that the reverse usage arose by confusion with phrases such as {{m|en|compound interest}}.

