===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[mate]], [[companion]], or [[associate]]. {{defdate|from 1590s}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XIII|20|[… these tears] Which weep the '''comrade''' of my choice,<br>⁠An awful thought, a life removed,<br>⁠The human-hearted man I loved,<br>A spirit, not a breathing voice.}}
## A [[companion]] in battle; fellow [[soldier]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=w:Antony Beevor|title=Arnhem: The Battle for the Bridges, 1944|publisher=Penguin Books|chapter=16|page=194|passage=Wierzbowski and his men were so exhausted that they could hardly stay awake, but they knew they could not abandon their wounded '''comrades'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|Q632316}}{{lb|en|communism|by extension}} A fellow [[socialist]], [[communist]] or other similarly politically aligned person. {{defdate|from 1884}}
#: {{ux|en|Hello, '''comrade'''. Are you going to the Communist Party meeting tonight?}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|year=1976
|journal=Survey of People's Republic of China Press
|issues=6086–6105
|issueurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=15hDAQAAIAAJ
|publisher=United States Consulate General (Hong Kong)
|issn=0253-0791
|oclc=2254330
|page=187
|text=Many '''comrades''' like Chang Te-fa, deputy secretary of the Party committee of Hsialuho commune of K'uantien county, precisely see the hope of the revolutionary cause in the youths.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:National Geographic|year=1989|month=May|volume=175|number=5|title=The Baltic: Arena of Power|author=Priit J. Vesilind|page=604
|text="How is it, '''comrades'''?" the admiral shouted through a bullhorn at each ship. "Rah, rah, rah!" the sailors shouted back in unison.}}
# {{senseid|en|communist title}} {{lb|en|communism}} {{non-gloss|A non-hierarchical title, functionally similar to "Mr.", "Mrs.", "Miss", "Ms." etc, in a communist, socialist, or rarely in an Islamist state.}} {{defdate|from 1884}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Comrade''' Lenin inspired our people to undertake great works.}}

