===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|concluden}}, borrowed from {{der|en|la|conclūdō|conclūdere|t=to shut up, close, end}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kənˈkluːd/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-conclude.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːd|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[end]]; to [[come to an end]].
#: {{ux|en|The story '''concluded''' with a moral.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1749|author=Henry Fielding|title=The History of Tom Jones, a Foundling|passage=He inveighed against the folly of making oneself liable for the debts of others; vented many bitter execrations against the brother; and '''concluded''' with wishing something could be done for the unfortunate family.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring to an end; to close; to finish.
#* {{RQ:Bacon War with Spain|passage=I will '''conclude''' this part with the speech of a counsellor of state.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring about as a result; to effect; to make.
#: {{ux|en|to '''conclude''' a bargain}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|V|iv|passage=if we '''conclude''' a peace}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To come to a [[conclusion]], to a final [[decision]].
#: {{ux|en|From the evidence, I '''conclude''' that this man was murdered.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=a. 1694|author=w:John Tillotson|title=The Advantages of Religion to Societies
|passage=No man can certainly '''conclude''' God's love or hatred to any person by anything that befalls him.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To make a final determination or judgment concerning; to judge; to decide.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1717|author=w:Joseph Addison|title=Metamorphoses
|passage=But no frail man, however great or high, / Can be '''concluded''' blest before he die.}}
# {{lb|en|often|passive}} To shut off; to restrain; to limit; to [[estop]]; to [[bar]].
#: {{ux|en|The defendant is '''concluded''' by his own plea.}}
#: {{ux|en|A judgment '''concludes''' the introduction of further evidence.}}
#* {{RQ:Hale Mankind|passage=If therefore they will appeal to revelation for their creation they must be '''concluded''' by it.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To shut up; to [[enclose]].
#* {{RQ:Hooker Laws
|passage=The very person of Christ [was] '''concluded''' within the grave.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To include; to comprehend; to shut up together; to embrace; to [[confine]].
#* {{quote-book|year=1601|en|author=w:Ben Jonson|title=w:The Poetaster|section=act IV, [https://www.hollowaypages.com/jonson1692poetaster.htm scene VIII] originally but scene VII in Gifford’s 1816 edition volume II|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/worksbenjonsonw02giffgoog/page/n503|page=493|passage=Banisht the Court? Let me be banisht Life;<br/> Since the chief end of Life is there '''concluded''':<br/>Within the Court is all the Kingdom bounded,<br/>And as her sacred [[sphere|Sphear]] doth comprehend<br/>Ten thousand times so much, as so much Place<br/>In any part of all the Empire else;<br/>So every Body, moving in her Sphear,<br/>Contains Ten thousand times as much in him,<br/>As any other, her choice Orb excludes.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Romans|11|32||For God hath '''concluded''' all in unbelief.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Galatians|3|22||The Scripture hath '''concluded''' all under sin.}}
# {{lb|en|logic}} to [[deduce]], to [[infer]] {{gloss|develop a causal relation}}

